SM(591,{"n":"Hebrews 2.1-3","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Hebrews 2.1-3.mp3","d":2648.5,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.88,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.16,"When we get started, in just a moment, we'll be picking up at uh the beginning of chapter two of St."],[27.6,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews."],[30.32,"And um we've been uh well, we went through chapter one."],[37.76,"So and we had previous to that we had the uh long introduction, which by the time people are hearing this recording, they'll be able to go back and listen to that recording."],[47.76,"Uh but um for today, um we saw the main things we saw as we went through chapter one was first and foremost, we kind of emphasizeed we saw sort of the technique that's being us here in St."],[63.16,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, that it doesn't sort of follow the same format as St."],[67.4,"Paul's epistles. And that this is why, of course, so many people, beginning with the Church Fathers, have questioned whether St."],[75.8,"Paul himself wrote it."],[77.64,"At least wrote it in the same sense."],[83.4,"But we also talked about how the argument is made, including by me, that uh there's a genre difference here."],[93.0,"That this isn't really an epistle in the sense of this being first and foremost a letter."],[98.36,"This is actually more like a homily or a sermon uh that's been sent somewhere."],[103.82,"So it's been sent the way a letter would be sent, but that it's actually in its genre and its shape and its structure, it's more like a sermon or a homily."],[113.86,"Specifically a sermon or a homily on uh Psalm 110 or 109 uh in the Greek numbering, uh, if you're looking at it in the Orthodox Study Bible or somewhere else that follows the Greek numbering."],[127.78,"Um and so the emphasis in the in the first chapter in terms of the content, we saw sort of that format that we talked about of uh how this was done within Second Temple Jewish circles in the first century, where even though you're you're sort of preaching about one text, you'll also introduce sort of other. texts"],[150.22,"You'll draw on texts from other places and bring them in parallel to sort of reflect how you should interpret the particular one that you're focusing on."],[160.3,"Rather than just sort of breaking down the grammar or like this and that that you you want to bring in other concepts from elsewhere in the scripture so that scripture is interpreting scripture."],[173.92,"The scriptures are interpreting themselves."],[177.44,"And so we saw there were a whole pile of other right passages in the first chapter."],[185.6,"And the main focus in terms of the content was talking about Christ as God the Son."],[196.57,"And then distinguishing him from the other beings, particularly the angels in this case, who are referred to as sons of God in the Old Testament."],[208.81,"So how is Christ different than the angels?"],[212.49,"And there were two ways that were focused on."],[217.29,"One was focused on more than the other."],[220.73,"One of them was that we saw that the Son is described as being eternal, meaning he was there at the beginning."],[227.77,"God created all things through him, meaning he would have created the angels through him and for him"],[235.61,"Whereas he is not spoken of as being created."],[240.79,"Or being something else through and for."],[245.57,"For some purpose for which he came into being, because he's not described as coming into being."],[250.29,"But the bigger focus was actually on Christ being different than and superior to the angels, because the Son uh is human."],[261.89,"And specifically the Messiah, that he was from the line of David."],[266.47,"That he was the reality behind the promises made to David."],[271.99,"And so just in chapter one of Hebrews, we got most of the contents of the seven ecumenical councils."],[280.49,"And they're their dogmatic decision."],[281.85,"Relationship We got the Trinity, at least we got the Father and the Son."],[287.75,"Christ is the Son."],[289.75,"And we got uh Christ is Christ's incarnation."],[296.37,"We got both that He is eternal God and that He is man."],[300.05,"He's the Son of God."],[301.25,"He's the Son of David."],[302.71,"The Messiah. Um, we got all that laid out real quickly."],[308.47,"Right took us a lot longer to talk about it than to read it, uh, in uh in Hebrews chapter one."],[314.31,"And so, uh, as we've talked about many times before, uh, these chapter and verse breaks are very late uh editions."],[324.71,"Uh so um we we tend to we're so used to them."],[330.53,"These were just added for convenience, most of them."],[334.29,"Uh the chapter, a lot of the chapter breaks go back to the the 12th century."],[339.09,"Not all of them, but a lot of them."],[341.25,"But the exact chapter and verse breaks we have now only go back to this middle of the 16th century."],[347.25,"Uh in one of the uh Robert Stephanus's early Greek New Testament printings."],[355.73,"Um, even though we've known them our whole lives, uh and they make it easy to point to a particular verse and refer to a particular verse, that's why he added them."],[365.73,"Uh we sh we have to be tocomes very easy, oh, there's a chapter break here, and it's convenient for me to have a place to stop a given Bible study."],[380.98,"After a certain amount of time."],[383.86,"But we shouldn't act like, oh, okay, so chapter one is about this and chapter two is about that, and sort of structure it in our heads around the chapter numbers."],[394.9,"Because sometimes they put a chapter number there because they detected a shift in topic or something."],[402.26,"But as we've seen as we've been going through, sometimes we look at where they put a chapter break, and it's just like, why?"],[408.18,"Why on earth would you put it there?"],[410.48,"Um, so um, don't read too much into that."],[414.2,"So all that is to say, really, so this is because this is a homily or a sermon."],[421.06,"There there weren't commercial breaks."],[423.08,"So it's not, there weren't like pauses, you know, where these chapter breaks are."],[427.4,"It was just one continuous argument."],[430.52,"So we have to kind of keep in our heads that even though obviously we're not going all through Hebrews in one sitting, because you know, we'd all die from lack of water and food."],[442.6,"Uh but um that this was presented at some point in one sitting."],[451.1,"In one continuous, this was one continuous uh argument."],[455.18,"So really we're we're we're continuing uh right from where we were before."],[462.5,"This isn't sort of now, okay, bullet point to episode two."],[466.94,"Um so uh with that said, uhless there are any other questions, comments, interesting journal articles somebody read about something, uh we'll pick up in St."],[483.42,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews chapter two, verse one."],[491.82,"Therefore, there's a therefore."],[497.2,"So, therefore, this is referring to, this is what I meant."],[500.0,"We've got to keep in mind what we just read, we must give the more earnest heed to the things we have heard, lest we drift away."],[511.08,"Verse 2, for if the word spoken through angels proved steadfast, and every transgression and disobedience received a just reward, how shall we escape if we neglect so great a salvation?"],[524.28,"Which at the first began to be spoken by the Lord and was confirmed to us by those who heard him."],[532.96,"So he is setting up here a parallel, a comparison."],[539.86,"So he starts out by saying, therefore, because of everything we just said about the way in which the Son."],[546.84,"The way in which God the Son, the way in which Christ is superior to the angels, we have to give the more earnest heed."],[555.08,"We need to be be even more dedicated to it, lest we drift away."],[562.58,"And for there is because."],[564.1,"And so then he makes his comparison."],[565.78,"So on the one hand, you have the word spoken through angels, which proved steadfast, and every transgression and disobedience received a just reward."],[574.82,"What's that referring to?"],[577.3,"Believe it or not, that's referring to the Torah."],[580.02,"Oh, okay. That's referring to the Torah."],[583.03,"I was just wondering when, if ever, as I three transgression of disobedience receives each other."],[588.64,"So, um. Yeah, so uh this verse uh is one of many that creates a problem for some of our Protestant friends, because you could go back, forth, and sideways through the Torah itself, through Exodus and the giving of the law, through Deuteronomy, and the second laying out of the law, uh, and you will not find anywhere where it talks about angels being involved."],[621.72,"In fact, if anything, you find some things that sound like they would contradict that."],[627.56,"For example, you read in Exodus about Moses speaking to God face to face."],[633.18,"This makes it sound like there's intermediaries."],[638.58,"But uh even though if we're just going, if we go by alone, the uh text of Exodus, you won't get this."],[649.75,"The text of Exodus that St."],[651.83,"Paul read and received, and everyone else, the first century read and received, they received within a context."],[660.66,"They didn't just walk into a room one day and find a Torah scroll sitting there."],[666.66,"Oh, what's this?"],[667.86,"And read it."],[668.76,"And just go from that."],[670.22,"The Torah scroll, there's super expensive."],[673.04,"The Torah scroll was at your local synagogue."],[677.34,"And you didn't read it because most people weren't literate."],[680.23,"At least at first, no one was reading it at first, you know, their small children."],[684.31,"They're hearing it."],[686.79,"They're going to the synagogue and they're hearing it read and they're hearing it explained, and they're hearing it taught."],[693.29,"By teachers in the synagogue."],[697.61,"And so there are ways of interpreting it and ways of understanding it and ways of telling the story that get handed down from generation to generation in the synagogues in the context in which it's being taught and being expressed."],[713.13,"And one of those things is not really additional information."],[718.89,"So this tradition we're talking. about is not like a bunch of additional information"],[724.51,"Like now the the Pharisees had additional information that says they had additional rules."],[731.6,"Um, and those get codified later in rabbinic Judaism, centuries later, as what they call the oral Torah."],[740.22,"That's sort of this additional stuff."],[743.38,"And uh we find uh Christ critiquing sort of the early version of that additional stuff when he's going back and forth with the Pharisees."],[753.4,"And pointing out how some of their additional stuff contradicts the stuff that's there in print."],[759.58,"In writing. Not in print yet, but in writing."],[766.96,"But that's not, as some of our friends would want to read it, Christ putting down the idea of tradition as such."],[776.62,"He's talking about particular ones that they've come up with that contradict."],[781.75,"That are coming from outside."],[783.53,"That aren't that aren't from God."],[786.49,"So they're receiving it within the tradition, and part of that tradition was not, again, additional information about how the law was given to Moses, but it was a way of understanding what's happening when the law is given to Moses."],[800.87,"And it's a way of drawing together what's in the the scriptures and what surrounds the scriptures and the things that scriptures themselves are pointing to."],[811.17,"So, what do I mean by what the scriptures are pointing to?"],[814.37,"I'll give an example that will seem unrelated at first."],[818.61,"Uh back in the way back in the long ago time, there's so many young people around now."],[824.05,"I feel so old."],[825.89,"But when uh the the Mel Gibson movie, The Passion of the Christ, came out, uh, one of the ways that some folks, including some Orthodox folks, criticized that movie."],[838.29,"And I'm not saying the movie's above criticism."],[840.71,"But one of the ways it was criticized was that that people would say, Oh, it's so gory."],[846.39,"It's so bloody. depicting Christ's beating and crucifixion and all of these things"],[852.59,"And they would want to contrast that with, well, you look at the gospel accounts."],[859.55,"You look at Saints Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John, and their accounts, I mean, just, you know, he was taken away and crucified."],[867.4,"It doesn't go into all that gory detail."],[870.14,"It doesn't do all that."],[872.11,"The response to that, though, like I on the surface, that would seem like, well, yeah, that is very different."],[877.07,"What is with this weird obsession with all the blood and the gore."],[880.25,"But the original readers, first century Jewish readers who were reading the gospel, had seen people crucified."],[888.97,"It was a means of public. humiliation and execution"],[894.15,"So they had seen the blood of the gore."],[897.83,"They knew what that meant to get scourged by the Romans."],[901.03,"They had seen it happen to people."],[903.2,"They knew how bloody gore."],[904.72,"So you didn't have to like describe it all to them."],[907.04,"You just had to say he was taken away and crucified."],[909.2,"And they would know how horrible that was."],[913.81,"So they could just give a very spare description and everybody knew what they were talking about."],[919.49,"Now, I at least that there are people in the world who have seen someone crucified, unfortunately."],[926.99,"There are places where horrible things have happened, and people have seen horrible, gory things."],[930.59,"And those people, you don't need to lay out details and like re-traumatize them."],[936.07,"They know what it's about."],[937.75,"But for people like me who have lived a relatively sheltered life in that respect."],[942.73,"I haven't seen someone murdered."],[944.49,"I haven't seen someone beaten to death, I haven't seen these things."],[948.75,"God has spared me having to see those things."],[960.47,"I need the more detailed description because it's not part of my experience."],[964.15,"Okay So, in the same way, lots in lots of places in the Old Testament, things are referred to and alluded to that if you are someone in the ancient Near East, if you were someone in ancient Israel, or even from a culture surrounding ancient Israel, or later Judea, and their surroundings."],[986.21,"There are things that are alluded to."],[988.69,"Like pagan stories that are alluded to, or ways of seeing things, ways of understanding the world and reality, that kind of thing that just at the time everybody knew."],[999.25,"And you could just refer to casually."],[1002.29,"The book of Jonah could just refer casually to the pillars of the earth."],[1007.95,"And everyone at the time knew what that meant."],[1011.85,"And and you could just make a reference to one of the stories of Baal or something in the Prophet Elijah's contest with Baal."],[1020.17,"Baal And everybody knew who was."],[1020.49,"In the ancient world"],[1024.32,"He was the god of one of the major powers at the time, the Phoenicians."],[1028.32,"Um so you could just allude to these things."],[1031.44,"But but we now, in modern times, I dare say, correct me if I'm wrong, no one in this room has read the Baal cycle."],[1044.08,"I can safely say none of you have read it in Ugaritic."],[1047.81,"So you might read one of those, and I think most of us have, read a whole bunch of stories that involve Baal in the Old Testament, and Palsms and stuff that are alluding to those things, and not even realized it."],[1062.37,"Had no idea what it was talking about, because we've never read it."],[1065.95,"We don't have that context."],[1068.92,"So one of the main things tradition does is it preserves that original context."],[1076.78,"It preserves those things surrounding it so that you can read it properly."],[1081.82,"Right later on."],[1084.7,"And so this is what's happening here with this law being given by angels thing."],[1089.5,"So yeah, I got back there eventually."],[1093.66,"That's what's happening."],[1095.1,"Because the way in which the giving of the law is described in, and we'll we'll see more of this as we go through Hebrews."],[1104.54,"Um, the way this is described in in Exodus, so in Exodus, in the original description, the way it is described would have put certain images and certain ideas in the minds of the original readers and hearers."],[1121.03,"And so when they read about the construction of the tabernacle and its shape, they would have understood certain things."],[1126.87,"When they see Moses going up on the mountain into the cloud, they would understand certain things."],[1131.83,"When he's standing and talking to God face to face, they would have had an understanding of what that means to them."],[1139.37,"And that understanding is not necessarily immediately shared by us as modern American people reading it."],[1146.93,"So they would they under what they understood is that uh first of all that Mount Sinai, when God came to rest there."],[1158.62,"First of all, that him coming to rest there meant he was enthroned there."],[1163.75,"We don't necessarily read that."],[1165.35,"In fact, most people who have read Genesis 1 1 through 2 2, which is the story of creation, when God rests on the seventh day, uh have pictured that as him taking a break."],[1179.77,"Yes Like he just worked real hard all week, and so T G I F right"],[1184.51,"And so he right, and so he he decides to take a break."],[1189.01,"They haven't understood rest there to mean what it originally mean, which was he sat down on his throne."],[1192.29,"he. That's enthroned at the end of that story"],[1199.27,"To rule over what he's now created."],[1202.04,"So they understood when when God comes to rest on Mount Sinai, that means he's now enthroned there, which means that's now the mountain of God."],[1213.26,"It's not always the mountain of God."],[1214.62,"Later on, the mountain of God is Mount Zion."],[1216.38,"What's the temples built there?"],[1218.06,"Because that's the place where God has come to rest, meaning that's the place where he's come to be enthroned."],[1224.51,"Um I don't want to digress too much on this, but uh there's also a constant arguments and Ig spilled about where Mount Sinai historically. was"],[1236.55,"Uh I'm willing to argue that in different parts of the Bible, two or three different mountains, physical mountains are Mount Sinai."],[1246.39,"Right now, St."],[1247.51,"Catharines is at Mount Sinai."],[1249.99,"That's the mountain that's currently being Mount Sinai."],[1252.71,"Um, but the mountain of God is where God is, is the mountain where he is."],[1258.68,"And that's not identified."],[1261.64,"It is identified at various times with particular mountains that you can go to and visit."],[1268.14,"But it's not confined to that."],[1270.31,"There were Greek people who walked up to the top of Mount Olympus and saw that there weren't any gods hanging out there."],[1276.81,"We tend we tend to think that ancient people were really stupid."],[1281.41,"It's our default."],[1282.69,"They were just as smart as us."],[1284.13,"Many of them were smarter."],[1285.89,"So, it was it was more subtle than that."],[1288.61,"They understood that uh the mountain they pointed to, or Mount Zephon with Baal."],[1295.46,"That that those became the the the mountain of God or of the gods at certain times, for certain purposes in certain ways."],[1304.27,"Um but so God is enthroned on Sinai when he's gonna give the Torah to Moses."],[1312.45,"And that throne language actually comes up if you read closely in Exodus, where the Amalekites attack the Israelites."],[1319.33,"God says that his judgment is going to be against them because they've dared to lay a hand upon the throne of Yahweh."],[1325.71,"So that throne language even pops up."],[1327.76,"So he's enthroned there."],[1329.12,"If he's enthroned there, that means one of the other words, probably the second most common term for the mountain of God in the Old Testament is the Mount of Assembly, which is Harmoeid, or if you want to be fancy."],[1343.44,"If you're a real fancy lad about how you pronounce biblical Hebrew, it's Harmohed."],[1349.36,"There you go."],[1350.64,"Uh that's but uh uh, and you may have heard from that pronunciation, that's what gets called Armageddon in the book of Revelation, but we'll get there."],[1360.72,"Uh, it's not Megiddo."],[1362.24,"Anyway, um Harmoed, the mountain of assembly."],[1364.24,"Har is the Hebrew word for mountain."],[1364.56,"Moed is assembly"],[1370.44,"And uh that's also the term that the pagans used for those mountains."],[1374.94,"For them, it was this is where the council of the gods."],[1377.74,"You've got one god who's the king of the gods, and then you've got the other gods, who sort of assemble there."],[1383.53,"Mount Olympus, Mount Ziphon for the Baal worshippers."],[1387.69,"Um, but that term gets used in the Old Testament, but it's the place where Yahweh, the God of Israel, is enthroned, and then assembled around him are the angels."],[1402.95,"The angelic beings."],[1404.31,"We see a scene like this at the beginning of the book of Job."],[1407.85,"God sits and he's got the angels on his right hand and on his left hand, and they're gathered in the assembly."],[1414.46,"So that means this is now."],[1418.07,"The mountain of assembly."],[1419.35,"This is the place of meeting."],[1421.03,"And Moses is invited to come up into that assembly."],[1426.77,"Into, you know, what you would call in a pagan context the council of the gods."],[1433.01,"Actually, the Old Testament calls it that."],[1435.01,"But we don't, because people get squeamish, so we call it the divine council, which means the same thing."],[1439.57,"But uh so it's with the angelic beings, the cherubim, the seraphim, and we see that scene over and over again in Isaiah, in Jeremiah, in Ezekiel."],[1451.17,"It's actually put out put forth that way in the book of Jeremiah"],[1451.67,"This is sort of the mark of a prophet later on."],[1456.87,"The person is a pro who's a prophet is the person who has been invited to come and stand in that council."],[1462.73,"So when Moses is there talking to God face to face, if we have preserved traditionally this vision of it, then we understand that, he's there in the council with the angels."],[1481.0,"And so it's delivered to him by the angels."],[1484.44,"And this is dramatized."],[1486.6,"Probably the most prominent piece of Second Temple Jewish literature that dramatizes this is what's called the Book of Jubilees, which is probably the most important extra canonical Old Testament book that nobody ever reads."],[1507.04,"And I say that because First Enoch is probably more important, but people read that."],[1514.16,"Nobody reads the Book of Jubilees."],[1517.44,"Unless you're one of our Ethiopian friends, for whom it's part of their Old Testament."],[1522.32,"So they do read it sometimes."],[1524.08,"I don't even think they read it a lot, though, but they have to read it sometimes."],[1528.04,"Um the Book of Jubilees uh is basically in a written form a lot of these traditions, a lot of these traditional understandings. a lot of this context"],[1542.04,"And so, like when Josephus writes his history of the Jewish people, he's cribbing from the Book of Jubilees."],[1549.72,"And he treats the Book of Jubilees like it's a historical document."],[1554.28,"Like, oh, this is additional historical information besides like the the text of the Torah itself."],[1563.26,"That's filling in blank."],[1564.3,"So he very much treats it in this context way, and this is Josephus in the first century AD treating it that way."],[1570.94,"That's how they that's how they understood it."],[1573.34,"Um, but it dramatizes this, and it has in particular the angel of the presence."],[1581.86,"Who is sort of the angel of Yahweh, the angel of the Lord figure."],[1584.06,"of But that gets connected to the Son Man in Daniel, the way Hebrews has been talking about God the Son."],[1585.46,"But we'll talk more about that another time."],[1595.44,"But so we see this idea that Moses is there in this divine council with the angels, alluded to when he receives the Torah, and that the Torah is therefore received sort of through this council."],[1609.34,"Through these angels."],[1611.1,"Even though it's nowhere in the Old Testament explicitly."],[1614.6,"It was so well preserved as context by the time you get to the first century AD, Saint Stephen refers to it in the sermon he gives before he's stoned to death."],[1626.76,"He talks about uh Moses having received the law through angels."],[1627.24,"It's here in Hebrews, and Saint Peter makes a reference to it"],[1634.93,"So this is one of those things that everybody knew."],[1639.83,"Like we talked about the most recent one of the other one of these we talked about was uh Janus and Jambris when St."],[1645.51,"Paul wrote to Timothy about it."],[1646.95,"Just everybody knew that was the name of the Pharaoh's magicians."],[1651.91,"Janus and Jambris, that's their names."],[1654.21,"Because it's been preserved as part of this larger context."],[1658.05,"Okay. So this is our first example."],[1660.79,"He's talking about the Torah."],[1662.31,"He's talking about how everyone understood Moses goes up into into, stands before God's throne, and the council, receives, through. the angels the Torah"],[1672.53,"And the Torah ends, the end of the book of Deuteronomy, has what?"],[1681.22,"Deuteronomy 28 through 30, it's today I set before you life and death, blessings and curses, choose."],[1690.56,"And it lays out if you if you walk in my ways, you keep the commandments I have given you, your crops will be fertile."],[1697.84,"'lls the You have sons and daughter and grandchildren and live long in land I'm giving you, etc."],[1700.64,"etc And then the other side, uh, if you do not walk in my ways, if you do not give me my commandments, and you go after other gods and you right and you do all these other things, then right, the sky will become like iron and the earth will become like bronze, which is bad if you're a farmer."],[1726.65,"And uh, you know, all of these things will happen, and you'll end up, you know, uh getting exiled from the land, and a foreign nations will conquer you, and etc."],[1736.81,"etc. etc. So there's these blessings and curses."],[1739.21,"And so that's what's being referred to here, this example."],[1742.67,"So we had the Torah before, the old covenant."],[1746.75,"It's given by given through angels."],[1750.64,"And uh there's these blessings and curses attached to it."],[1756.61,"And if you transgress it, you're going to receive the curses."],[1759.09,"So this was a serious thing."],[1761.57,"Well then, if we've established that Christ, that God the Son, is greater than the angels."],[1770.58,"Then how shall we escape if we neglect so great a salvation?"],[1776.18,"Which at the first began to be spoken by the Lord and was confirmed to us by those who heard him."],[1784.18,"This is coming from God Himself."],[1790.36,"Not through angels, but through those who are through the apostles."],[1795.5,"Through the apostles."],[1796.54,"Now notice he's put the apostles in the place of the angels."],[1801.86,"Um we do all the time and may not notice."],[1806.82,"But we say their sound has gone out into all the world, and there are words to the ends of the universe, which is the Prochemenon, and shows up several other places every time we have a feast of the apostles in general, or of any of the apostles in particular."],[1820.18,"If you go back to the Psalm that that verse is from, it's talking about the stars of the sky."],[1825.7,"It's talking about the angels."],[1827.14,"It's what's comparing the apostles to the angels."],[1830.74,"And that's that's what's happening here."],[1832.82,"Um, but because Christ is greater than neglecting the salvation that comes from Christ."],[1839.22,"Neglecting the new covenant, therefore has a far worse."],[1846.75,"Imagine what those curses are like."],[1848.76,"But that's the flip side of imagine what those blessings are like."],[1851.18,"The blessings are so much greater."],[1852.38,"So the salvation offered is so much greater than anything offered by the Torah."],[1858.08,"Which, as we've talked about many times before, was sort of the sin management system to get Israel through to Christ coming."],[1865.41,"Um if that carried these sanctions, then if we neglect this, this is going to be even worse."],[1874.69,"Um I do need to comment uh in verse three."],[1881.97,"So one of the arguments made by the people who want to say St."],[1886.21,"Paul's not behind this is that, oh see, he says, we."],[1891.87,"First spoken by the Lord and was confirmed to us, to we, to us, by those who heard him."],[1896.91,"And they say, well, St."],[1897.95,"Paul never says he he received anything from the apostles."],[1902.35,"He always says he got the gospel straight from Christ himself."],[1906.31,"So two things, two things on that."],[1908.95,"Number one, that's not strictly speaking true."],[1913.19,"Remember 1 Corinthians, when St."],[1915.19,"Paul talks about the Eucharist, he says, I passed on to you what I also received."],[1922.95,"That Christ on the day."],[1924.41,"What's he talking about there?"],[1926.33,"He's talking about he received how to celebrate the Eucharist from the people who were there with Christ when Christ did it."],[1938.9,"So St. Paul very much does talk exactly this way in one of his nobody contests this."],[1945.94,"Nobody, the wildest liberal of the world, doesn't think St."],[1949.06,"Paul didn't write 1 Corinthians."],[1950.82,"Okay. So uncontested."],[1953.38,"So St. Paul does speak this way."],[1955.94,"But also, that's that's missing this parallel that's being made between the apostles and the angels."],[1963.08,"Which is the point of them being mentioned here."],[1968.97,"A normal person reading this or hearing this in a normal context would not be struck in that verse by, oh, this person is saying that they received it the gospel secondhand."],[1984.5,"That is not what's being said at all."],[1988.8,"There's a contrast being made through between the Torah being received through angels."],[1995.74,"And then the gospel being the new covenant being received through Christ of the Apostles."],[2002.86,"That's the that's the parallel that's being made."],[2005.82,"Doesn't Paul think of himself as an apostle?"],[2008.78,"Yes. Therefore, we and us would refer to himself among the apostles."],[2015.82,"Well, no, because the the way this is phrased is confirmed to us by those who heard him."],[2017.26,"that So they they would say, see, the the writer is lumping himself in with the hearers, as people who received it from the apostles."],[2031.06,"That that's their argument."],[2032.5,"But my point is that's not what he's doing."],[2035.62,"That's very clearly not what he's talking about, this passage."],[2038.74,"He's making a par he's holding two things up in parallel."],[2043.11,"He's not defending or denying his own apostleship in this in this section."],[2050.63,"So this is a bugbear of scholarship."],[2061.19,"Okay I've got a PhD so I can say this."],[2064.99,"Um where one of the signs of particular forms of mental illness is that you ascribe to things, you sort of fetishize things, you ascribe to things an undue pertinence and meaning."],[2082.61,"That isn't really there."],[2085.02,"So you see a sign or you see a color or you see a number and you go off on what sounds like a conspiracy theory."],[2092.56,"This is a sign that X, Y, Z, da da da da da."],[2096.18,"That's that's and uh even though that is one of the signs of several forms of mental illness, that's also something that scholars do all the time."],[2106.87,"And so uh you can attest also."],[2110.29,"And you it if you stare at Greek grammar of a particular verse enough, like you you start to lose all sight of context, you start to lose sight of like how language works, that when people talk and they say a sentence, they usually only mean one thing, they don't mean like all of the possible ways you could interpret that sentence by itself and with the content like I'm just meaning one thing."],[2145.02,"And if you did that in normal real life."],[2147.5,"Like if I suddenly said, Oh, I need to go, I'm not feeling well"],[2154.38,"And you sat and said, He needed to go, he's not feeling well."],[2159.82,"What does it mean to not feel well?"],[2163.91,"What is it?"],[2164.71,"He needed to go."],[2166.31,"He didn't just want to go."],[2167.75,"It wasn't just a decision."],[2168.87,"He was compelled to go."],[2170.56,"And you started to everyone would look at you like you were a crazy person."],[2174.48,"Isn't this what the signal time is?"],[2176.8,"If I say something and somebody misinterprets what I've said, I heard the words, and the words could mean what they said."],[2184.62,"But that's not what I meant, so I correct it."],[2186.71,"But this is even more pathological because you're sitting there deliberately dwelling on it."],[2188.15,"Trying to draw everyone will look at you like a crazy person"],[2194.73,"They'd be like, no, he he just he wasn't he wasn't feeling good, so he had to, so he left."],[2200.31,"Like, you know, that's it, it just means what it says."],[2204.07,"It just means what it means."],[2205.35,"And so um you get a lot of that."],[2210.64,"If you have somebody who's a New Testament scholar, the New Testament is not that big."],[2216.4,"It's not that long."],[2219.52,"And if you spend years of your life staring at it at the original Greek, you very quickly start drawing things out that aren't there in that same kind of way."],[2232.45,"That are kind of detached from reality"],[2238.63,"And so there always has to be sort of this interpretive check of like, well, we'll wait."],[2248.71,"What is the author talking about right now?"],[2254.47,"Does the way I want to apply this or understand this have anything to do with what he's actually talking about?"],[2262.07,"This may seem obvious, but we've seen places."],[2266.65,"We've seen places."],[2268.81,"I'll go ahead and pick out our Calvinist friends again."],[2271.37,"We've seen places where like a word like election gets used."],[2276.75,"And all of a sudden, it doesn't matter what St."],[2281.23,"Paul was talking about."],[2284.07,"What his topic is."],[2285.99,"He used the word elect, and so now we're off into this whole theological thing."],[2291.81,"Which he can't possibly, you know, if you stop for a second and look at the guy, he can't possibly have been talking about that."],[2299.33,"It would make no sense."],[2300.69,"It would be the bizarrest digression ever."],[2306.03,"I mean, Romans 9 being exhibit A."],[2308.45,"He's he's talking about why so many of the Judean people have not accepted Jesus as their Messiah."],[2317.41,"And then decides to talk about, oh, by the way, God picked people before he created the world to go to heaven and hell."],[2327.71,"Okay, I'll stop picking on Calvinists."],[2329.71,"But you get a little bit of that here."],[2331.88,"So someone someone comes to Hebrews, and they're not trying to read and understand Hebrews."],[2338.84,"They're not trying to get, at least at this moment."],[2341.66,"They may in other moments, but in this moment, they're not looking at that."],[2345.26,"They're not looking at what is St."],[2346.94,"Paul or what is the author saying."],[2350.4,"They're definitely not saying what is God trying to say to me right now through Hebrews."],[2354.0,"They're definitely not doing that."],[2355.46,"But they're approaching it like, who wrote Hebrews?"],[2358.82,"That's the question in their brain."],[2360.5,"Who wrote Hebrews?"],[2362.54,"I'm looking for pieces of evidence that St."],[2367.1,"Paul wrote it or he didn't write it."],[2370.22,"And so when you come to this verse, you don't ask, what's he talking about?"],[2374.3,"How does this verse fit with the verse before it and the verse after it?"],[2377.18,"You just say, aha."],[2379.1,"In this verse, he says us about the people who heard the apostles."],[2385.34,"Saint Paul wouldn't do that."],[2389.9,"Add it to my list of proofs that St."],[2392.54,"Paul didn't write it."],[2393.58,"I'm going to publish my journal article."],[2396.02,"Uh so, that's the approach."],[2399.21,"And so the problem is with that approach."],[2402.11,"You're trying to mine information out of the Bible that isn't there."],[2405.51,"The reason I'm taking the time for this digression is this is the main thing that leads to people coming up with oddball Bible interpretations."],[2414.55,"And it starts a lot of arguments that you don't have to start."],[2419.08,"We were talking about this before, but there is nothing in Genesis that is trying to tell you how old the earth is."],[2426.68,"There just isn't."],[2428.04,"There's not a place."],[2429.24,"Because it would be very easy if it was."],[2431.86,"There could just be a verse saying, this is how old the earth is."],[2437.62,"Like, that's really easy."],[2440.95,"Or you could approach Genesis to say, I'm gonna figure out from Genesis how old the Earth is."],[2447.95,"And then you come up with one date, and then you read the Greek version, you come up with another date, and then you read the Dead Sea Scroll, there's some slightly different numbers, and you come up with another date, and then you can all argue about it for the rest of eternity."],[2461.15,"And you haven't actually gotten into anything of what Genesis was actually trying to tell you about God creating the world."],[2472.52,"Because you went in with a question you wanted answered."],[2476.52,"And and and over and over again."],[2480.84,"I I'm, you know, I'm really mad that the Pope is selling indulgences to pay for this building project in Rome."],[2489.53,"And so I'm gonna comb through Galatians to try and find places that will tell me that this is wrong."],[2498.85,"So I can go and hold a public debate and win against the Pope's, the cardinal the Pope sends."],[2505.14,"To argue with me."],[2507.1,"Wrong approach. St."],[2509.26,"Paul was not talking about anything 16th century popes were doing."],[2513.02,"He didn't know there would be 16th century popes at the time he wrote it."],[2517.26,"I mean he knew later, because he's still around, but he's a saint."],[2521.34,"But at the time, that's not what he was writing about."],[2526.04,"And so you're you're if you ask the wrong questions, you're gonna get the wrong answers."],[2531.72,"And so that's what's happening."],[2534.56,"That's what's happening here."],[2535.76,"And the only reason I took the time to do that here is because anytime I mention St."],[2541.28,"Paul writing Hebrews, this is usually the first thing people mention."],[2545.28,"If they're one of the people who likes to argue about who wrote Hebrews."],[2550.96,"So it was pertinent here."],[2552.88,"But this applies generally."],[2554.48,"This applies to everything in the scriptures."],[2556.72,"We have to come to the scriptures wanting to learn from the scriptures what the scriptures can teach us."],[2564.26,"And that's not irrelevant to our current time and place and the debates we're having and the struggles we're in and all those things."],[2571.0,"The scriptures have something to say to us about all those things."],[2575.66,"The scriptures are written for us, but they aren't written to us."],[2576.62,"St Paul didn't know I was going to exist."],[2584.66,"He didn't know what an America was."],[2588.42,"He had no concept of politics equivalent to ours."],[2591.38,"He had no concept of any of these things equivalent to ours."],[2594.66,"He's writing about something in his time and his place, to people he knows usually."],[2601.46,"And now we want to understand how that applies to our situation and what we can learn from it as we address the situations in our own time and place."],[2612.34,"Paul is doing here is making this parallel analogy"],[2613.02,"And so what St."],[2618.43,"Between the way the Torah was received and the way the new covenant received. in Christ is"],[2626.07,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God."],[2633.43,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2638.79,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.88,"lv":2,"h":"Recap of chapter 1: a homily on Psalm 110","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Hebrews 2:1</b>; prior: chapter 1 and long <b>introduction</b> episode</li><li class=\"d0\">Technique in Hebrews: <b>not the format of Paul's epistles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why many, <b>beginning with the Church Fathers</b>, question whether Paul wrote it (at least in the same sense)</li><li class=\"d0\">Argument (including his): <b>genre difference</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not really a letter - more a <b>homily/sermon</b> sent the way a letter is sent</li><li class=\"d1\">specifically a sermon on <b>Psalm 110 (109 in Greek numbering)</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Second Temple Jewish</b> format: preach one text, bring in <b>other texts in parallel</b> to show how to interpret it</li><li class=\"d1\">not breaking down grammar; <b>scripture interpreting scripture</b></li></ul>"},{"t":185.6,"lv":3,"h":"Chapter 1 content: Christ and the angels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Main focus: Christ as <b>God the Son</b>, distinguished from <b>angels</b> (called sons of God in OT)</li><li class=\"d0\">Two ways Christ differs:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>eternal</b> - there at the beginning; all things created through and for him, so angels too; <b>not described as coming into being</b></li><li class=\"d1\">(bigger focus) <b>superior because the Son is human</b> - the <b>Messiah</b>, line of <b>David</b>, reality behind the promises</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Just in chapter one we got <b>most of the contents of the seven ecumenical councils</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Trinity</b> (Father and Son), <b>incarnation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">eternal God <b>and</b> man; Son of God, Son of David, Messiah</li><li class=\"d1\">\"took us a lot longer to talk about it than to read it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":314.31,"lv":2,"h":"Chapter and verse breaks; one continuous argument","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Chapter/verse breaks = <b>very late additions</b>, for convenience</li><li class=\"d1\">many chapter breaks go back to <b>12th century</b></li><li class=\"d1\">exact chapter+verse numbers: <b>mid-16th century</b>, <b>Robert Stephanus</b>' Greek NT printings</li><li class=\"d0\">Handy for pointing to a verse and for a stopping place in a Bible study</li><li class=\"d0\">But don't structure thinking around them - \"why on earth would you put it there?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews = homily: <b>no commercial breaks</b></li><li class=\"d1\">presented <b>in one sitting</b>, <b>one continuous argument</b> (\"we'd all die from lack of water and food\")</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not now, okay, bullet point to <b>episode two</b>\" - continuing right where we were</li></ul>"},{"t":466.94,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 2:1-3 - \"therefore\": give heed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:1</b> \"Therefore…\" - \"there's a therefore\" - keep in mind what we just read</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>give the more earnest heed</b> to what we have heard, <b>lest we drift away</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:2</b> \"if the word spoken through angels proved steadfast, every transgression received a just reward, how shall we escape if we neglect <b>so great a salvation</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:3</b> \"…which at the first began to be spoken by the Lord, confirmed to us by those who heard him\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Setting up a <b>parallel, a comparison</b></li><li class=\"d1\">because of everything about Christ's superiority to angels → <b>more dedicated</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"for\" = because</li></ul>"},{"t":565.78,"lv":3,"h":"The word spoken through angels = the Torah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One side: word spoken through angels, steadfast, every transgression punished</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>the Torah</b> - \"believe it or not\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem verse for some <b>Protestant friends</b></li><li class=\"d1\">search Exodus, giving of the law, Deuteronomy: <b>no mention of angels</b></li><li class=\"d1\">worse: Exodus has <b>Moses speaking to God face to face</b> - sounds like no intermediaries</li></ul>"},{"t":649.75,"lv":2,"h":"Scripture received within a tradition","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Text Paul and 1st c. readers received came <b>within a context</b></li><li class=\"d1\">didn't just find a scroll in a room - Torah scroll <b>super expensive</b>, at your <b>local synagogue</b></li><li class=\"d1\">most not literate; children <b>heard</b> it read, explained, taught by synagogue teachers</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ways of interpreting and telling the story</b> handed down generation to generation</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not additional information</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pharisees' extra rules = different: later codified in rabbinic Judaism as <b>oral Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ critiqued the <b>early version</b>, where it <b>contradicts the written text</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not Christ putting down <b>tradition as such</b>; only particular ones from <b>outside, not from God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Real tradition = <b>a way of understanding</b> what happens at the giving of the law</li><li class=\"d1\">draws together scriptures, what surrounds them, what they point to</li></ul>"},{"t":811.17,"lv":2,"h":"Example: The Passion of the Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Seems unrelated at first - <b>Mel Gibson's <i>Passion of the Christ</i></b></li><li class=\"d0\">Criticized (even by some Orthodox) as <b>gory, bloody</b></li><li class=\"d1\">contrast: gospels just say \"he was taken away and <b>crucified</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Original 1st c. readers had <b>seen crucifixions</b>, knew <b>Roman scourging</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you just had to say he was taken away and crucified\" - <b>spare description</b> enough</li><li class=\"d0\">Some people today have seen horror - don't need details, <b>no re-traumatizing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">For someone <b>sheltered</b> like him (never seen murder or a beating) - \"God has spared me\" - <b>need the detailed description</b>, not part of experience</li></ul>"},{"t":964.15,"lv":3,"h":"Allusions the ancients knew: Baal, pillars of the earth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">OT constantly alludes to things everyone in the <b>ancient Near East</b> knew</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pagan stories</b>, ways of seeing the world and reality</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jonah</b> casually refers to <b>pillars of the earth</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Elijah's contest</b> with <b>Baal</b> - god of the <b>Phoenicians</b>, a major power</li><li class=\"d0\">\"No one in this room has read the <b>Baal cycle</b>\" - \"in Ugaritic\"</li><li class=\"d1\">we read stories and Psalms alluding to Baal \"and not even realized\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Main thing tradition does</b>: preserves the <b>original context</b> so you can read it properly later</li><li class=\"d0\">→ what's happening with <b>law given by angels</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1089.5,"lv":2,"h":"How Exodus would have read: God enthroned on Sinai","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Exodus' description put <b>certain images and ideas</b> in original hearers' minds</li><li class=\"d1\">tabernacle's shape; Moses going up into the cloud; standing <b>face to face</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not immediately shared by \"modern American people\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God came to rest on Sinai</b> = he was <b>enthroned</b> there</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Genesis 1:1-2:2</b>: God \"rests\" on seventh day - pictured as <b>taking a break, \"TGIF\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">original meaning: <b>sat down on his throne</b> to rule over what he'd created</li><li class=\"d0\">→ Sinai = now the <b>mountain of God</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1213.26,"lv":3,"h":"The mountain of God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not always Sinai: later <b>Mount Zion</b> - temple built where God came to rest/be enthroned</li><li class=\"d0\">Argues two or three <b>different physical mountains</b> called Sinai in different parts of the Bible</li><li class=\"d1\">today <b>St. Catherine's</b> at the current Mount Sinai</li><li class=\"d0\">Mountain of God = <b>wherever he is</b>; identified with visitable mountains at times, not confined</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Greeks</b> climbed Olympus, saw no gods - \"we think ancient people were really stupid… many were <b>smarter</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Mount Zephon</b> with Baal - the mountain of the gods at certain times, for certain purposes</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Throne language</b> in Exodus: Amalekites attacked → judgment because they \"laid a hand upon the <b>throne of Yahweh</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1329.12,"lv":3,"h":"Mount of Assembly and the divine council","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Second most common term: <b>Har Moed</b> (<i>Harmoed</i>) = <b>mount of assembly</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>har</i> = mountain, <i>moed</i> = assembly; the <b>\"Armageddon\"</b> of Revelation - \"not Megiddo\" (we'll get there)</li><li class=\"d0\">Pagan term too: <b>council of the gods</b> - king of the gods + assembled gods (Olympus, Zephon)</li><li class=\"d0\">In OT: <b>Yahweh enthroned</b>, <b>angels assembled</b> around him</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Job</b> opening: angels on his right and left hand</li><li class=\"d0\">OT calls it the council; we say <b>\"divine council\"</b> because people get squeamish - same meaning</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>cherubim, seraphim</b>; scene recurs in <b>Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mark of a prophet</b>: invited to <b>stand in that council</b> (Jeremiah)</li></ul>"},{"t":1462.73,"lv":3,"h":"Moses in the council: the Book of Jubilees","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moses talking to God face to face = <b>in the council with the angels</b></li><li class=\"d1\">law <b>delivered to him by the angels</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Book of Jubilees</b> dramatizes this - \"most important extra-canonical OT book that nobody reads\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 Enoch</b> more important but people read it</li><li class=\"d1\">Ethiopian Church: part of their OT, \"they have to read it sometimes\"</li><li class=\"d1\">written form of these <b>traditional understandings, context</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Josephus</b> \"cribbing\" from Jubilees - treats it as <b>historical document</b> filling in blanks of the Torah</li><li class=\"d0\">Has the <b>angel of the presence</b> = angel of Yahweh/Lord figure</li><li class=\"d1\">connects to the <b>Son of Man in Daniel</b>, as Hebrews on God the Son - \"another time\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1595.44,"lv":3,"h":"Everybody knew: Stephen, Jannes and Jambres","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Torah received <b>through the angels</b>, though nowhere explicit in OT</li><li class=\"d1\">context so well preserved by 1st c. AD</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Stephen</b> refers to it in his sermon before stoning: Moses received law through angels</li><li class=\"d1\">also here in Hebrews; <b>St. Peter</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>Jannes and Jambres</b> (Paul to Timothy) - everybody knew the names of <b>Pharaoh's magicians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">preserved as part of the larger context</li></ul>"},{"t":1658.05,"lv":2,"h":"Torah's blessings and curses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First example: <b>Torah</b> - Moses stands before God's throne and council, <b>receives it through angels</b></li><li class=\"d0\">End of <b>Deuteronomy 28-30</b>: \"today I set before you <b>life and death, blessings and curses, choose</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">keep commandments → <b>crops fertile</b>, sons, daughters, grandchildren, long life in the land</li><li class=\"d1\">don't, go after other gods → <b>sky like iron, earth like bronze</b> (\"bad if you're a farmer\")</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>exile</b>, foreign nations conquer</li><li class=\"d0\">So old covenant: given through angels, with <b>sanctions</b> → transgress = <b>curses</b>; \"a serious thing\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1761.57,"lv":2,"h":"2:3 - how shall we escape? Apostles in the angels' place","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">If Christ (God the Son) is <b>greater than the angels</b> → <b>how shall we escape</b> if we neglect so great a salvation?</li><li class=\"d0\">Spoken first by the Lord, <b>not through angels</b> but <b>through the apostles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">apostles put <b>in the place of the angels</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we say it all the time: <b>\"their sound has gone out into all the world\"</b> - <i>prokeimenon</i> at feasts of the apostles</li><li class=\"d1\">Psalm it's from is about <b>stars of the sky</b>, the <b>angels</b> → apostles compared to angels</li><li class=\"d0\">Neglecting <b>new covenant</b> = <b>far worse</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"imagine what those curses are like\" - flip side: <b>blessings much greater</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Torah = <b>sin management system</b> to get Israel through to Christ's coming; if it carried sanctions, this is worse</li></ul>"},{"t":1874.69,"lv":2,"h":"The \"we\" of verse 3 and who wrote Hebrews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Argument against Paul: \"confirmed <b>to us</b> by those who heard him\" - lumps himself with hearers</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul says he got the gospel <b>straight from Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two responses:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 Corinthians</b> on Eucharist: <b>\"I passed on to you what I also received\"</b> - received from those with Christ; nobody contests 1 Cor</li><li class=\"d1\">argument <b>misses the parallel</b> of apostles and angels</li><li class=\"d0\">A normal hearer wouldn't hear \"received the gospel secondhand\"</li><li class=\"d1\">contrast: Torah through angels / new covenant <b>through Christ and the apostles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not defending or denying <b>his own apostleship</b> here</li></ul>"},{"t":2050.63,"lv":3,"h":"Scholarly over-reading","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">A \"bugbear of scholarship\" - \"I've got a PhD so I can say this\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Sign of some <b>mental illness</b>: fetishize things, ascribe <b>undue meaning</b> to a sign, color, number → conspiracy theory</li><li class=\"d1\">scholars do it all the time</li><li class=\"d0\">Stare at the <b>Greek grammar</b> of a verse → lose sight of context and <b>how language works</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people usually mean <b>one thing</b>, not all possible readings</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: \"I need to go, I'm not feeling well\" - parsing \"needed\", \"compelled\" → \"everyone would look at you like a <b>crazy person</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">NT is short; years in the Greek → drawing out things <b>detached from reality</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Needs an <b>interpretive check</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2262.07,"lv":2,"h":"Ask the author's question, not yours","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>What is the author talking about right now?</b> Does my application fit that?</li><li class=\"d0\">Calvinist friends: word <b>\"election\"</b> → off into a whole theological thing regardless of Paul's topic</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Romans 9</b> exhibit A: why Judeans haven't accepted Jesus as Messiah → \"by the way, God picked people before the world to go to heaven and hell\"? \"bizarrest digression ever\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews reader asking <b>\"who wrote Hebrews?\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hunting evidence Paul did/didn't write it - not \"what is God saying to me through Hebrews\"</li><li class=\"d1\">verse says \"us\" → \"add to my list of proofs… publish my journal article\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Result: <b>mining information that isn't there</b> → <b>oddball interpretations</b>, needless arguments</li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis: <b>nothing telling how old the earth is</b> - come with that question, get a different date from Hebrew, Greek, DSS, \"argue about it for the rest of eternity\"</li><li class=\"d1\">never get at what Genesis says about <b>God creating</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hypothetical: hunting <b>Galatians</b> to beat the Pope over <b>indulgences</b> - Paul wasn't writing about 16th-c. popes</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Ask the wrong questions, get the wrong answers</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2534.56,"lv":3,"h":"Written for us, not to us","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Took the digression because mentioning Paul writing Hebrews <b>always</b> draws it - but applies to all scripture</li><li class=\"d0\">Come wanting to <b>learn what scripture can teach</b></li><li class=\"d1\">relevant to our time, debates, struggles</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Written for us, but not written to us\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul didn't know I'd exist, <b>America</b>, or politics like ours</li><li class=\"d1\">writing about his time and place to people he usually knows</li><li class=\"d0\">→ then ask how it applies to our situation</li><li class=\"d0\">Here Paul/the author is making a <b>parallel analogy</b>: how the <b>Torah</b> was received vs. how the <b>new covenant</b> is received in Christ</li></ul>"}]});
